import { BytesReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesReaderCopy = BytesReaderCopy {
  summary: "HexDecodeError distinguishes an odd hexadecimal input length from an invalid character. The input length and an invalid character’s byte offset are different pieces of information.",
  taskTitle: "Explain why hexadecimal input was rejected",
  purpose: "Instead of merely rejecting pasted hexadecimal, tell the caller whether to revisit the digit count or look for an invalid character. Read the failure returned by hex.decode and display the input length or invalid position.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript’s Uint8Array.fromHex throws on invalid input, so try/catch can display Invalid hex. This example compares success and failure for the same inputs. It does not add code to manufacture Seseragi’s categories or positions from exceptions, so the failure messages differ.",
  benefit: "Seseragi returns a named reason with a numeric payload. Display the input byte length and zero-based input byte position separately without parsing a textual error message. Right containing valid bytes is also distinct from Left containing only a reason.",
  setup: "hex aliases std/bytes/hex. decode reads String and returns Either<HexDecodeError, Bytes>. Either is one of two results: Left with a failure reason or Right with a successful value. Bytes is an immutable byte sequence.",
  after: [
    "fn read input: String -> String defines a function from input to a display string. fn introduces the definition, : String describes the argument, and -> String describes the returned value. hex.decode input calls a function by placing its argument after a space.",
    "The outer match separates Right content from Left error. Right content writes the bytes as hexadecimal with hex.encode and uses + to add Decoded: . Left error uses another match to distinguish the reason inside error.",
    "hex.OddHexLength length is the branch that names an odd input length length. hex.InvalidHexDigit { offset } names the invalid character’s position offset. { offset } reads the named value carried by that reason. These two branches handle every HexDecodeError reason.",
    "In a string enclosed in backticks, ${length} and ${offset} insert the numbers into the display text. The expression after each branch’s -> supplies its result. println (read input) prints the display string returned by read.",
    "00FF succeeds and is displayed in lowercase as 00ff. The input 0 fails for odd length, 00xz fails on the non-digit x, and é0 fails for an odd length measured in UTF-8 bytes. The number displayed has a different meaning for the length and digit checks.",
  ],
  details: [
    "The Int carried by OddHexLength Int is the input’s UTF-8 byte length, not an invalid position. decode checks even length first. Although é0 has two visible characters, its three UTF-8 bytes cause a length failure.",
    "After the length check passes, a character outside ASCII 0–9, a–f, and A–F produces InvalidHexDigit. Its offset counts the input’s UTF-8 bytes from zero. In 00xz, x is at position 2. The input é alone has length 2, so it produces an invalid-digit error at position 0.",
    "Do not directly manipulate the private representation of HexDecodeError. Use the public reason names OddHexLength and InvalidHexDigit in match. Assuming that error always has an offset would miss the reason that carries only a length.",
    "0x00 and 00 00 are not directly accepted as hexadecimal input. Check the input format and handle any permitted prefix or separator separately before decode. A failure does not provide partial Bytes.",
    "Successful Bytes is not necessarily text. Separately use decodeUtf8 from std/text before treating it as text. These reasons describe hexadecimal representation errors, not the meaning or safety of the data.",
  ],
  nodeNote: "The inspected Node 24 runtime has no fromHex()/toHex(). The Node/Bun example imports { Buffer } from \"node:buffer\" and checks even length and ASCII hexadecimal digits first, because Buffer.from(input, \"hex\") alone may truncate at invalid input. This alternative also compares success with rejection without reproducing HexDecodeError categories or numeric payloads.",
}
